SLIDE 05 | 1:20 | The pipeline scales without rebuilding the corpus
DELIVERY: Walk across the diagram in seven stages. Slow down on structured state and incremental scale.

This diagram shows the complete data journey. On the left are the source documents: contracts, amendments, exhibits, rate sheets, and reference files. In ingestion, Auto Loader detects new or changed files, OCR converts scanned pages to text, and layout extraction preserves tables and page structure. Processing then chunks the text, extracts metadata, recognizes clauses and entities, normalizes inconsistent rate terminology, and links amendments. The result is structured contract data: providers, contracts, rates, obligations, compliance terms, deadlines, and events. That data is stored in three complementary forms - processed files in the lake, governed relational tables, and vector embeddings for semantic retrieval. The serving layer combines search, natural-language processing, ranking, business rules, calculations, and confidence checks. Only after all of that does the application receive intelligence-ready data. Underneath are sixteen orchestrated stages and forty-five governed Delta tables. Checkpoints, file hashes, distributed OCR, incremental MERGE, and changed-unit re-indexing mean we process what changed rather than rebuild everything. More than 10,500 documents are implemented today; the same pipeline supports incremental expansion toward 30,000-plus.

TRANSITION: But scalable extraction still does not answer the hardest question: which version governs?

